Delete soft-deleted dependents with their parent in the clean-up

`barbican-manage db clean` fails with MariaDB error 1451 whenever the
cutoff of a run falls between the `deleted_at` of a soft-deleted
parent and the later `deleted_at` of one of its soft-deleted child
rows. `SoftDeleteMixIn.delete` (barbican/model/models.py) sets
`deleted_at = timeutils.utcnow()` on the parent, flushes it, and only
then calls `_do_delete_children`. `Secret._do_delete_children` calls
`delete` on every store metadata row, user metadata row, encrypted
datum and container secret, `Order._do_delete_children` on both order
metadata tables, and each call takes a `utcnow()` of its own. The
secret and container controllers soft-delete the consumers after the
parent. `deleted_at` is `sa.DateTime`, in MariaDB a `datetime` without
fractional seconds, so a delete that crosses a second boundary stores
second S on the parent and S+1 on a child. `clean_command` computes
one cutoff per run; `cleanup_softdeletes` selects each child table by
the child's own `deleted_at`, and `cleanup_parent_with_no_child`
selects the parent by the parent's. With the cutoff in [S, S+1) the
child stays, and the `DELETE` of the parent fails on the foreign key.
oslo.db raises `DBReferenceError`, which `_run_batch` does not retry.
The expired-secret pass leaves the same gap, minutes wide:
`_soft_delete_expired_secrets` stamps the secrets with one
`utcnow()`, and `_soft_delete_expired_secret_children` stamps their
children with a later one. The hourly clean-up of the C5C3/playground
cluster failed nine times this way between 2026-11-19 and 2026-11-23,
six times on `secret_store_metadata` and three times on
`secret_user_metadata` (C5C3/cobaltcore#1861).

Reproduced on 2026-11-24 on one mariadb:12.3 (12.3.3) container with
the schema of `barbican-manage db upgrade`, the rows created through
the models and their `deleted_at` set with `UPDATE`, then
`clean.cleanup_all` with the cutoff at S + 0.5 s:

  row  parent at S         rows at S+1 s                unpatched  patched
  1    secret              5 secret_store_metadata,     1451       8 rows
                           2 secret_user_metadata
  2    secret, its 5       2 secret_user_metadata       1451       8 rows
       store metadata
  3    order               1 order_barbican_metadata    1451       2 rows
  4    container           1 container_consumer_        1451       2 rows
                           metadata
  5    container           its container_secret,        1451       2 rows
                           soft-deleted with its
                           secret, and that secret
  6    expired secret      none                         1451       1451

Rows 1 and 2 fail on `secret_store_metadata` and `secret_user_metadata`
with the messages of the playground, word for word, rows 3 to 5 on
`order_barbican_metadata`, `container_consumer_metadata` and
`container_secret` (`container_id`). In row 5 the secret stays. Row 6
is a secret with a past expiration, soft-deleted by
`soft_delete_expired_secrets`, whose `secret_consumer_metadata` row
keeps `deleted = 0`; `cleanup_all` with the cutoff two days later fails
on that row on every run, before and after the patch. Without the
patch a second run with the cutoff at S+2 s cleans rows 1 to 5, as the
retried Job does in the playground. Of 3,000 secrets with five store
and two user metadata rows each, deleted through `Secret.delete()`
with one commit each, 11 (0.37 %) stored a later second on their store
metadata, on a local database without MaxScale in the path.

Decided on 2026-11-24 (C5C3/cobaltcore#1861):

- The parent pass deletes the leftover child rows in the parent's
  batch. Skipping a parent until its children are gone would keep a
  parent whose child is never soft-deleted for good, without any run
  failing.
- All three parent passes of `cleanup_all` are covered, also the pairs
  that were derived from the code and never seen in the playground
  (rows 3 to 5).
- Only soft-deleted child rows (`deleted` true) go with their parent,
  whatever their `deleted_at`. A child row that was never soft-deleted
  keeps failing the run with 1451, so row 6 stays as it is.
- The same patch corrects the count of a failed run.

A retry of the batch that hits 1451 cannot help: the cutoff is fixed
for the run, so every retry reads the same rows. The retried Job of
the playground succeeds because its cutoff is minutes later.

`cleanup_parent_with_no_child` gains `dependent_keys=()`, the foreign
key columns of the dependent models. For each of them, in the given
order and before it deletes the parents, `_delete` calls the new
`_delete_soft_deleted_dependents`. It reads the ids of the rows with
`deleted` true that reference the parents of the statement, with no
`deleted_at` filter, and deletes them by id, at most
`_IDS_PER_STATEMENT` per statement. It reads before it deletes, so the
usual batch, which finds no row, takes no lock in the child table, and
a delete by primary key stays on the plan `_IDS_PER_STATEMENT` was
introduced for. The dependents share the transaction, the commit and
the `DBDeadlock` retry of their parents. A batch that deletes
dependents logs `Clean-up batch for Secret/no-Order deletes 1
soft-deleted SecretStoreMetadatum entries ahead of their own cutoff,
with the parents they reference` at INFO, and the INFO line of a pass
with `dependent_keys` ends with `, their soft-deleted dependents
included`, because its count includes them. Without `dependent_keys`
the pass and its line are unchanged.

`cleanup_all` passes `OrderPluginMetadatum.order_id` and
`OrderBarbicanMetadatum.order_id` to the order pass;
`EncryptedDatum.secret_id`, `SecretUserMetadatum.secret_id`,
`SecretStoreMetadatum.secret_id`, `ContainerSecret.secret_id` and
`SecretConsumerMetadatum.secret_id` to the secret pass; and
`ContainerConsumerMetadatum.container_id` and
`ContainerSecret.container_id` to the container pass. These nine are
every foreign key into the three parents whose rows are soft-deleted
apart from the parent, and each column has an index.
`ContainerSecret.container_id` is among them because
`Container._do_delete_children` reaches its container secrets through
a relationship that filters `ContainerSecret.deleted != True`, so a
row soft-deleted with its secret outlives the delete of the container
(row 5). `SecretACL` and `ContainerACL` are hard-deleted in the
transaction that soft-deletes their parent and need no key.

The extra cost is one indexed read per dependent key and statement of
a parent batch. On the rig with 40,000 secrets, every fourth
soft-deleted, five store and two user metadata rows each with the
`deleted_at` of their secret, one `cleanup_all` with the cutoff a day
later deletes 80,000 rows. The sum of the `Handler_read%` counters of
`SHOW GLOBAL STATUS` over that run rose from 410,213 to 430,273:
20,000 more `Handler_read_key`, one per deleted secret and non-empty
dependent table, and `Handler_read_next` unchanged at 319,840.
`EXPLAIN` of the read on `secret_store_metadata` for 500 secret ids
shows `range` on `ix_secret_store_metadata_secret_id`.

A failed run also reported fewer rows than it committed.
`clean_command` adds the rows of a pass when the pass returns, so
every batch the failing pass committed before it raised was missing
from `Failed to clean up soft deletions in database. %s entries were
committed before the failure.` and from `Cleaning of database affected
%s entries`. After the failed run of row 2 the five store metadata
rows were gone, while the log read `0 entries were committed before
the failure`. A module-level `_rows_committed` beside `_batch_pause`
counts the rows of each batch `_run_batches` gets back from
`_run_batch`, so a batch that wrap_db_retry runs again counts once.
`clean_command` sets it to 0 when a run starts and reports it in both
lines when the run fails. The success path keeps the return values of
the passes, and the texts of both lines stay.

Tests: no upstream test moves. `test_db_cleanup_raise_integrity_error`
still expects `DBReferenceError` for a child row that was never
soft-deleted. The patch adds `_PARENT_AT`, `_LATER` one second later,
`_BETWEEN` half a second later and the helper `_soft_delete_at` to
barbican/tests/cmd/test_db_cleanup.py, the test method
`_record_statements`, and twelve tests: the secret pass deletes five
kinds of dependents set to `_LATER` with their secret at `_PARENT_AT`
and logs one `Clean-up batch` line per table; the order pass and the
container pass do the same, and the secret of the container secret
stays; a secret past the cutoff takes its own dependent, while the
dependents of secrets that are live, not past the cutoff or still
have an order stay;
`cleanup_parent_with_no_child` without `dependent_keys` raises
`DBReferenceError` as before; a live dependent fails the batch on its
first occurrence, and after the rollback every row of it exists; a
batch retried after `DBDeadlock` deletes parent and dependents and adds
its six rows to `_rows_committed` once; five dependents go in
statements of 2, 2 and 1 ids and a live one stays; a parent without
dependents issues no `DELETE` on a dependent table; a run whose secret
pass fails after three committed transport keys reports 3 in both
lines, and the next run counts from zero; a run whose transport key
pass fails in its second batch reports the 2 rows of its first; a run
that fails before its first batch reports 0. The two tests on the
call without `dependent_keys` and on the live dependent pass without
the change to clean.py; the other ten fail.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_barbican.sh (Test 15) asserts in
the built image that `cleanup_all` with the cutoff between the two
values deletes a secret together with its store and user metadata set
a second later, and that a run failing on a live store metadatum hands
`LOG.exception` the 4 rows its batches committed.

The twins under patches/barbican/2026.1/ and patches/barbican/2026.2/
carry the same diff and differ in their Applies-to line only.

Applies to barbican 22.0.0 (2026.1), on top of 0001, 0002 and 0006.

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

